:"post_office","la":33.594598,"lo":130.389937,"d":1485},{"n":"南日本銀行","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":33.59578,"lo":130.411647,"d":1488},{"n":"西日本シティ銀行","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":33.591554,"lo":130.400495,"d":1495},{"n":"福岡銀行","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":33.594679,"lo":130.389343,"d":1513},{"n":"博多栄町郵便局","g":"essentials","k":"post_office","la":33.600068,"lo":130.415281,"d":1517},{"n":"誠十字病院","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":33.601067,"lo":130.415725,"d":1520},{"n":"杏林会林病院","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":33.602373,"lo":130.416281,"d":1535},{"n":"Hakata Port Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.604333,"lo":130.39775,"d":221,"ds":"Hakata Port Tower is a 103 metre high lattice tower with an observation deck in a height of 73.5 metres in Hakata-ku, Fukuoka, Japan. Hakata Port Tower was built in 1964.","wpu":"Hakata Port Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/%28JPN-Fukuoka%29_Hakata_Port_Tower_2024-11-14_-_1.jpg/960px-%28JPN-Fukuoka%29_Hakata_Port_Tower_2024-11-14_-_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fukuoka Prefectural Museum of Art","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.597242,"lo":130.39875,"d":870,"ds":"Fukuoka Prefectural Museum of Art (福岡県立美術館, Fukuoka kenritsu bijutsukan) opened in Fukuoka, Japan, in 1985. The collection focuses upon artists from Fukuoka Prefecture and Kyūshū more generally, and includes works by Koga Harue. The Museum's precursor, the Fukuoka Prefectural Cultural Hall (福岡県文化会館), which combined art museum with library, opened on 3 November 1964.","wpu":"Fukuoka Prefectural Museum of Art","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Fukuoka_Museum_of_Art.JPG/960px-Fukuoka_Museum_of_Art.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fukuoka Asian Art Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.595155,"lo":130.405851,"d":1221,"ds":"Fukuoka Asian Art Museum (福岡アジア美術館, Fukuoka Ajia Bijutsukan) is a museum of Asian art that opened in Hakata, Fukuoka, Fukuoka Prefecture, Japan in 1999. The collection of modern and contemporary art comprises some three thousand works from twenty-three countries.","wpu":"Fukuoka Asian Art Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/%E5%8D%9A%E5%A4%9A%E3%83%AA%E3%83%90%E3%83%AC%E3%82%A4%E3%83%B3%E3%83%A2%E3%83%BC%E3%83%AB_by_TAKASHIMAYA.JPG/960px-%E5%8D%9A%E5%A4%9A%E3%83%AA%E3%83%90%E3%83%AC%E3%82%A4%E3%83%B3%E3%83%A2%E3%83%BC%E3%83%AB_by_TAKASHIMAYA.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sōfuku-ji (Fukuoka)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.605719,"lo":130.413731,"d":1274,"ds":"Sōfuku-ji (崇福寺) is a Rinzai temple in Hakata-ku, Fukuoka, Japan. Its honorary sangō prefix is Yokodakezan (横岳山). The temple was founded by the monk Tan'e (湛慧) in Dazaifu in 1240, but was moved to its present location in 1600 after it became the Kuroda family temple.","wpu":"Sōfuku-ji (Fukuoka)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Gate_of_Sofukuji_Temple_2.JPG/960px-Gate_of_Sofukuji_Temple_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Our Lady of Victory Cathedral, Fukuoka","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.591,"lo":130.3974,"d":1575,"ds":"The Our Lady of Victory Cathedral (Japanese: 聖母の勝利司教座聖堂) also called Daimyomachi Church is a religious building affiliated with the Catholic Church located in the city of Fukuoka, Japan. It is dedicated to Our Lady of Victory.","wpu":"Our Lady of Victory Cathedral, Fukuoka","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Daimyomachi_Catholic_Church.jpg/960px-Daimyomachi_Catholic_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Shōfuku-ji (Fukuoka)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.597028,"lo":130.414111,"d":1579,"ds":"Shōfuku-ji (聖福寺) is a Buddhist temple located in the Gokushōmachi neighborhood of Hakata, Fukuoka, Japan. It belongs to the Rinzai school Myōshin-ji-branch of Japanese Zen. Its sangō prefix is Ankokuzan (安国山).","wpu":"Shōfuku-ji (Fukuoka)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Shoufukuji01.jpg/960px-Shoufukuji01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kushida Shrine","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.592972,"lo":130.410472,"d":1652,"ds":"Kushida-jinja (櫛田神社) is a Shinto shrine located in Hakata-ku, Fukuoka, Japan. Dedicated to Amaterasu and Susanoo, it is said to have been founded in 757. The Hakata Gion Yamakasa festival is centred on the shrine.","wpu":"Kushida Shrine","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Kushidajinjafukuoka01.jpg/960px-Kushidajinjafukuoka01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Terumo Shrine","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.597222,"lo":130.376389,"d":2351,"ds":"Terumo Shrine (光雲神社) is a Shinto shrine in Fukuoka City. Latterly located in Nishi Park in the Chūō-ku ward of the city, the shrine and all contents were destroyed in 1945; a reconstruction in modern materials was erected in 1966.","wpu":"Terumo Shrine","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Fukuoka_Terumo_shrine_2010A.jpg/960px-Fukuoka_Terumo_shrine_2010A.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Rakusui-en","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.586972,"lo":130.413389,"d":2357,"ds":"Rakusui-en (楽水園) is a garden in Hakata-ku, Fukuoka, Japan. It was originally built in 1906 for Shimozawa Zenemon Chikamasa, a Hakata merchant. In 1995, Fukuoka City overhauled the structure and reopened it as a Japanese garden where visitors can experience a tea ceremony; with four tea rooms (two of which are available for use by general visitors) it is often used for tea ceremonies and related training.","wpu":"Rakusui-en","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/Rakusui-en_04.jpg/960px-Rakusui-en_04.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hakozaki Shrine","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.614444,"lo":130.423333,"d":2402,"ds":"Hakozaki Shrine (筥崎宮, Hakozaki-gū) is a Shintō shrine in Fukuoka .","wpu":"Hakozaki Shrine","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Hakozakigu01.jpg/960px-Hakozakigu01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Diocese of Fukuoka","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.583333,"lo":130.4,"d":2409,"ds":"The Diocese of Fukuoka (Latin: Dioecesis Fukuokaensis, Japanese: カトリック福岡教区) is a Latin Catholic diocese of the Catholic Church located in Fukuoka in the Ecclesiastical Province of Nagasaki in Japan.","wpu":"Diocese of Fukuoka","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Daimyomachi_Catholic_Church.jpg/960px-Daimyomachi_Catholic_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sumiyoshi Shrine (Fukuoka)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.585906,"lo":130.413703,"d":2473,"ds":"Sumiyoshi Shrine (住吉神社) is a Shinto shrine in the Sumiyoshi neighborhood of Hakata-ku, Fukuoka in Fukuoka Prefecture, Japan. It is the ichinomiya of former Chikuzen Province. The main festival of the shrine is held annually on October 13.","wpu":"Sumiyoshi Shrine (Fukuoka)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Sumiyoshi-jinja_%28Fukuoka%29_haiden-1.JPG/960px-Sumiyoshi-jinja_%28Fukuoka%29_haiden-1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fukuoka Castle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.585,"lo":130.383056,"d":2721,"ds":"Fukuoka Castle (福岡城, Fukuoka-jō) is a Japanese castle located in Chūō-ku, Fukuoka, Japan. It is also known as Maizuru Castle (舞鶴城 Maizuru-jō) or Seki Castle (石城 Seki-jō). Completed in the early Edo period for tozama daimyō Kuroda Nagamasa, it has been designated a National Historic Site of Japan in 1957.","wpu":"Fukuoka Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Fukuoka_Castle_Simonohasi_Otemon_gate.JPG/960px-Fukuoka_Castle_Simonohasi_Otemon_gate.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fukuoka Mosque","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.62,"lo":130.426944,"d":3001,"ds":"The Fukuoka Mosque, officially the Fukuoka Masjid Al Nour Islamic Culture Center (Japanese: アン ヌール イスラム文化センター 福岡マスジド), is a mosque located in Hakozaki, Higashi-ku, in the city of Fukuoka, in the Fukuoka Prefecture of Japan. Opened on 12 April 2009, it was the first mosque on the island of Kyūshū. Fukuoka Mosque serves the approximately 1,000 Muslims in the prefecture.","wpu":"Fukuoka Mosque","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/aa/Fukuoka_masjid_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Ōhori Park","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.585861,"lo":130.37625,"d":3060,"ds":"Ōhori Park (大濠公園, Ōhori-kōen) is a park in Chūō-ku, Fukuoka, Japan and a registered Place of Scenic Beauty.","wpu":"Ōhori Park","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/%E5%A4%A7%E6%BF%A0%E5%85%AC%E5%9C%92_%283360365578%29.jpg/960px-%E5%A4%A7%E6%BF%A0%E5%85%AC%E5%9C%92_%283360365578%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fukuoka Japan Temple","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.572618,"lo":130.391704,"d":3681,"ds":"The Fukuoka Japan Temple (福岡神殿, Fukuoka Shinden) is a temple of the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints located in Fukuoka, Japan. It was announced on May 7, 1998, and later dedicated on June 11, 2000, by church president Gordon B. Hinckley. It became the church's 88th operating temple, the second in Japan (after Tokyo), and the first in southern Japan.","wpu":"Fukuoka Japan Temple","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/Fukuoka_Japan_Temple_by_tylermhawkins.jpeg/960px-Fukuoka_Japan_Temple_by_tylermhawkins.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fukuoka City Museum","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.589768,"lo":130.353085,"d":4663,"ds":"Fukuoka City Museum (福岡市博物館, Fukuoka-shi hakubutsukan) opened in Fukuoka, Japan, in 1990. The permanent exhibition, which tells the history of Fukuoka, is arranged in eleven sections, including those focussing upon the King of Na gold seal (National Treasure), the Kuroda clan, and the Hakata Gion Yamakasa.","wpu":"Fukuoka City Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/Fukuoka_City_Museum_2.JPG/960px-Fukuoka_City_Museum_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fukuoka Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":33.59325,"lo":130.351472,"d":4680,"ds":"Fukuoka Tower (福岡タワー, Fukuoka Tawā) is a 234-metre (768 ft) tall tower located in the Momochihama area of Fukuoka, Japan. It is the tallest seaside tower in Japan. The highest observation deck at 123 metres has a 360° view of the surrounding area, being most visited at sunset.","wpu":"Fukuoka Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Fukuoka_Tower_Before_Sunset.jpg/960px-Fukuoka_Tower_Before_Sunset.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Fukuoka (福岡市, Fukuoka-shi; [ɸɯ̥.kɯꜜ.o.ka, -kɯ.o.kaꜜ.ɕi] ) is a designated city in the Kyushu region of Japan and the capital city of Fukuoka Prefecture. The city is built along the shores of Hakata Bay, and has been a center of international commerce since ancient times. The area has long been considered the gateway to the country, as it is the nearest point among Japan's main islands to the Asian mainland.","wpu":"Fukuoka","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Fukuoka_Skyline_of_Seaside_Momochi.jpg/960px-Fukuoka_Skyline_of_Seaside_Momochi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}